The transition was not a violent tear, but a gentle fold, a stepping from one reality into another. One moment, the five of them stood on sterile metal decking; the next, your boots sank into warm, crushed shells that whispered underfoot. The air changed, filling lungs with the salt-heavy perfume of tropical blossoms—frangipani and night-blooming jasmine—and the clean, sharp scent of the open ocean. A low, melodious thrumming seemed to vibrate up from the ground itself, the heartbeat of the slumbering volcano. Before them lay the Azure Atoll, a necklace of verdant islands draped around a lagoon so impossibly turquoise it seemed lit from within.

